WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.778 --> 00:00:08.540
Hello.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.970
This is 6 Minute English from

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:11.410
BBC Learning English.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:12.311
I’m Neil.

00:00:12.311 --> 00:00:13.500
And I’m Beth.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.490
When I was a boy, I wanted to be

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.720
a fireman when I grew up.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:18.720
How about you, Beth?

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.720
Did you have

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:20.720
any childhood dreams?

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.550
I wanted to be an astronaut

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.470
and fly to the Moon…

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.140
When we’re young most of us have

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.920
big dreams and plans for the future.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.160
Unfortunately, as we grow up these

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.870
childhood dreams often get lost in the

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.979
adult world of jobs, money,

00:00:36.979 --> 00:00:38.519
families and careers.

00:00:38.519 --> 00:00:39.989
But not for everyone…

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:42.699
Daisy, from New Zealand, and, Herman,

00:00:42.699 --> 00:00:44.210
from Argentina are two

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.609
people who decided

00:00:45.609 --> 00:00:48.059
to follow their childhood dreams.

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:49.059
They

00:00:49.059 --> 00:00:51.429
wanted the world to become a utopia – a

00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:53.969
perfect, ideal society where everyone is

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:56.850
happy and gets along with each other.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.739
In this programme, we’ll be hearing how

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:01.510
Daisy and Herman made their dreams

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.629
come true – not by changing the world,

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:06.250
but by changing themselves.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.670
And, as usual,

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.430
we’ll be learning some new vocabulary too.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.630
But before that I have a question

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.420
for you, Beth.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.420
Following your dreams can

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.070
be tough, but not following them can leave

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.320
you regretting all the things you wanted

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.280
to do but didn’t.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.130
In 2012, Australian nurse,

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.450
Bronnie Ware, wrote her bestselling book,

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:28.731
The Top Five Regrets of the Dying, after

00:01:28.731 --> 00:01:31.270
interviewing terminally ill patients about

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:32.700
their life regrets.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.380
So, what do you think

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.560
their top regret was?

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.560
Was it:

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.950
a) I wish I hadn’t worked so hard?

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.650
b) I wish I had followed my dreams?

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:43.650
or

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.140
c) I wish I’d made more money?

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.300
Well, I’ll guess it’s b) they wish they

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.580
had followed their dreams.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.580
OK, Beth.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:51.909
I’ll reveal the correct answer

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:53.750
at the end of the programme.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.200
The first dreamer we’re going to meet lives

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.490
in Riverside, a peace-loving community in

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.409
New Zealand where everyone shares

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:02.790
everything.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:04.140
Riverside members work for

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.540
the community’s businesses, including

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.110
a farm, a hotel and a café.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.110
All the money they

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.040
earn is collected and shared

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.780
between everyone equally.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.209
Daisy, who was born in East Germany,

00:02:17.209 --> 00:02:20.180
joined Riverside in 2004.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:21.180
Here she explains

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.879
her belief in sharing to BBC

00:02:22.879 --> 00:02:26.560
World Service programme, The Documentary.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.290
What I think I always believed in is that

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.790
the sharing of resources can provide

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.700
a group of people with quite a great

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.749
advantage, but it doesn’t matter how

00:02:35.749 --> 00:02:37.749
many hours you work or what work

00:02:37.749 --> 00:02:39.159
you do, everyone is getting the

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:40.819
same amount.

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:41.819
And that is something

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:43.420
that many people outside of

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.650
Riverside struggle with, and where

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.040
we’re often getting this ‘communism’

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.769
label attached to us, because it’s so…

00:02:51.769 --> 00:02:54.849
it seems so outlandish for people.

00:02:54.849 --> 00:02:57.760
Riverside isn’t a communist community.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.590
In fact, people with many different political

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.200
views live there.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.909
But Daisy says that local

00:03:03.909 --> 00:03:06.109
people struggle with the idea that

00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:08.029
everything is shared.

00:03:08.029 --> 00:03:09.120
If you struggle with

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.430
an idea, you find it difficult to

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.499
accept or think about it.

00:03:13.499 --> 00:03:15.849
Daisy also says some local people

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:18.269
call Riverside outlandish –

00:03:18.269 --> 00:03:20.299
strange and unusual.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:22.239
Our second group of dreamers are

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:24.730
a family - the Zapps.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:26.549
In 2000, childhood

00:03:26.549 --> 00:03:29.069
sweethearts, Herman and Candelaria Zapp,

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:31.309
bought a vintage car and set off

00:03:31.309 --> 00:03:33.529
from Argentina to travel around

00:03:33.529 --> 00:03:37.209
the world with less than 3.500 dollars

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:38.860
in their pockets.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.299
Twenty-two years and

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:43.090
three children later they have visited

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.510
over a hundred countries, meeting

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.950
with countless people and

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.049
experiences on the way.

00:03:49.049 --> 00:03:51.430
Here, Herman Zapp explains to BBC

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.770
World Service’s, The Documentary, how

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.409
following his dream has

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:57.139
changed him for the better.

00:03:57.139 --> 00:04:00.260
I am so happy with the Herman there is now,

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.519
that I know now – not the one who

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:04.439
wanted to conquer the world, but the one

00:04:04.439 --> 00:04:06.199
who was conquered by the world.

00:04:06.199 --> 00:04:07.199
I learn

00:04:07.199 --> 00:04:08.890
so much from people, and it’s

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:10.449
amazing how the more you meet people,

00:04:10.449 --> 00:04:12.269
the more you know stories, how

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:14.620
much more humble you become because

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.060
you notice that you are a beautiful, tiny

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:20.460
piece of sand, but a very important piece

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.440
of sand like everyone is, right?

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.440
After many years travelling, meeting

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.430
new people and hearing their stories,

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.380
Herman is more humble – not proud or

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:32.760
arrogant.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:33.940
He no longer wants to

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.870
conquer the world – to control it by force;

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.860
rather, he has been conquered

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.040
by his experiences.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.230
Herman compares himself to a beautiful

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.760
but tiny piece of sand and uses the phrase

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:49.270
'a grain of sand' to describe things which

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:51.950
are insignificant in themselves, but

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:54.160
at the same time are an important

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.700
part of the whole.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.120
Daisy and Herman are rare examples

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.590
of dreamers who followed their dream

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:02.760
and found a happy life, lived without

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.760
regret – which reminds me

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:05.880
of your question, Neil.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:11.360
Yes, I asked about Bronnie Ware’s book,
The Top Five Regrets of the Dying.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.420
What do you think the number one regret was,
Beth?

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:18.050
I guessed it was b) not following your dreams.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.640
Which was the right answer!

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.640
Not having

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.500
the courage to follow your dreams was

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.560
listed as the top life regret.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:27.560
At least we

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:28.740
have people like Daisy and Herman

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.990
to remind us dreams can come true!

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:33.790
OK, let’s recap the vocabulary from

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.480
this programme, starting with

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.250
'utopia' – a perfect world where

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:39.660
everyone is happy.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.470
If you 'struggle with an idea',

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:43.570
you find it difficult to accept.

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:45.840
The adjective, outlandish, means

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.020
strange and unusual.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.560
'To conquer' something means

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.790
to control it by force.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:53.310
Someone who is 'humble' is

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.400
not proud or arrogant.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:57.700
And finally, the phrase 'a grain of sand'

00:05:57.700 --> 00:05:59.560
describes something which is both

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.690
insignificant yet somehow important.

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:04.952
Once again, our six minutes are up.

00:06:04.952 --> 00:06:05.719
Bye for now!

00:06:05.719 --> 00:06:06.690
Goodbye!

